WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.284
Suppose your program asks the user for a number,

00:00:04.284 --> 00:00:06.940
and the user types 42, in words.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:11.843
Your code calls int on that text, to turn it into a number.

00:00:11.843 --> 00:00:16.010
And the whole program stops, with a red error message.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.600
Can we do better than crashing?

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.394
In this part, we'll combine questions with and, or and not,

00:00:23.394 --> 00:00:27.350
and we'll build a safety net for lines that might fail.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:33.333
This is Lecture 3, part 2: logical operators, and try and except.

00:00:33.683 --> 00:00:39.203
At the end, we'll put the pieces together in a small program that works out tomorrow's date.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.981
The logical operators combine Boolean expressions into one.

00:00:43.981 --> 00:00:45.600
There are three of them.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.537
Not flips True and False.

00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:52.884
And is true only when both sides are true.

00:00:52.884 --> 00:00:57.230
Or is true when at least one side is true.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.208
So not True is False.

00:01:00.208 --> 00:01:02.926
False and True is False.

00:01:02.926 --> 00:01:05.870
And True or False is True.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:12.180
When an expression has several of them, not is applied first, then and, then or.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.756
Just like times comes before plus in arithmetic.

00:01:15.756 --> 00:01:18.140
And brackets override the order.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:24.794
So False and False or True means: first, False and False, which is False.

00:01:24.794 --> 00:01:27.730
Then False or True, which is True.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.008
With brackets around False or True, the or goes first, and gives True.

00:01:34.008 --> 00:01:36.464
Then False and True is False.

00:01:36.464 --> 00:01:38.920
Same words, different answer.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.521
Logical operators are what you need to test a range.

00:01:43.521 --> 00:01:47.783
Score greater than or equal to 60, and score less than 80.

00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:52.780
With a score of 75, both sides are true, so the whole thing is True.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:59.514
Python also lets you chain the comparisons: 60 <= score < 80.

00:01:59.514 --> 00:02:02.130
It reads just like maths.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.080
And or tests for alternatives: is the day Saturday, or is it Sunday?

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.763
And and or also short-circuit.

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:13.950
They stop as soon as the answer is known.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.801
If the left side of an and is false, the whole thing must be false,

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:23.084
so Python never looks at the right side.

00:02:23.084 --> 00:02:28.420
If the left side of an or is true, the right side is skipped too.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.117
That's useful as a guard.

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.123
Here x is 0.

00:02:32.123 --> 00:02:37.826
x not equal to 0 is False, so Python never computes 10 divided by x,

00:02:37.826 --> 00:02:41.180
which would be a division by zero error.

00:02:41.967 --> 00:02:43.551
Here's a classic mistake.

00:02:43.551 --> 00:02:49.567
You want to know whether the day is Saturday or Sunday, so you write: if day == 'Sat' or 'Sun'.

00:02:49.917 --> 00:02:51.554
The day is Monday.

00:02:51.554 --> 00:02:54.917
But the program still prints Weekend!

00:02:55.267 --> 00:02:59.946
That's because or joins two conditions, not two values.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:05.902
Python reads it as: day == 'Sat', which is False, or the string 'Sun'.

00:03:05.902 --> 00:03:09.305
And a string that isn't empty is truthy.

00:03:09.305 --> 00:03:12.027
So the condition is always true.

00:03:12.627 --> 00:03:19.907
Write the full comparison on both sides of the or: the day == 'Saturday', or the day == 'Sunday'.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.976
Now, errors.

00:03:21.976 --> 00:03:24.102
Some lines can fail.

00:03:24.102 --> 00:03:28.886
Int of the string '123' gives the number 123.

00:03:28.886 --> 00:03:32.500
But int of 'Hello bob' can't work.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.767
Python raises a ValueError.

00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:39.311
And if nothing handles it, the program stops right at that line.

00:03:39.311 --> 00:03:41.370
The lines after it never run.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.960
A try, except statement is a safety net, like the net under a tightrope walker.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.301
You put the risky line in the try block.

00:03:50.301 --> 00:03:53.367
If it works, the except block is skipped.

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:57.479
If it fails, Python jumps straight to the except block,

00:03:57.479 --> 00:04:00.470
and then carries on after the statement.

00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:04.319
Here's the program with the net in place.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:06.255
The string is 'Hello bob'.

00:04:06.255 --> 00:04:10.572
The conversion fails, so Python jumps to the except block,

00:04:10.572 --> 00:04:12.507
and sets the result to -1.

00:04:12.857 --> 00:04:16.817
First -1 is printed, and the program keeps going.

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:20.284
The second time, the string is '123'.

00:04:20.284 --> 00:04:24.750
The conversion works, so the except block is skipped,

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.687
and we get Second, 123.

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:29.501
Pause the video and predict.

00:04:29.501 --> 00:04:31.316
Here the string is 'Bob'.

00:04:31.316 --> 00:04:34.438
Which of Hello, There and Done are printed?

00:04:34.438 --> 00:04:36.907
And what is the result at the end?

00:04:37.457 --> 00:04:38.961
Print Hello runs.

00:04:38.961 --> 00:04:44.623
Then int of 'Bob' fails, so Python leaves the try block at once.

00:04:44.623 --> 00:04:46.657
Print There is skipped.

00:04:47.007 --> 00:04:50.640
The except block sets the result to -1,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.087
and the program prints Done, then -1.

00:04:54.437 --> 00:04:56.725
So keep your try blocks short.

00:04:56.725 --> 00:05:00.157
Then it's clear which line you're protecting.

00:05:00.934 --> 00:05:06.243
The most common use of try and except is reading a number typed by the user.

00:05:06.243 --> 00:05:10.574
Input always returns a string, and the user may type anything.

00:05:10.924 --> 00:05:15.641
If the user types 42, as digits, the conversion works,

00:05:15.641 --> 00:05:19.484
value is 42, and the program says Nice work.

00:05:19.834 --> 00:05:23.679
If they type 42 in words, int raises a ValueError.

00:05:23.679 --> 00:05:30.754
The except block sets value to -1, and the program says Invalid number, instead of crashing.

00:05:31.104 --> 00:05:36.264
Notice that the except line names the error it expects: except ValueError.

00:05:36.614 --> 00:05:37.605
That matters.

00:05:37.605 --> 00:05:44.614
A bare except, with no error name, catches every error, including the bugs in your own code.

00:05:44.964 --> 00:05:50.724
In this program, the name of the string is misspelt inside the try block.

00:05:50.724 --> 00:05:52.854
That's a NameError, a typo.

00:05:52.854 --> 00:05:56.404
But the bare except quietly turns it into -1.

00:05:56.754 --> 00:06:01.549
The input was fine, but the program says it was invalid,

00:06:01.549 --> 00:06:03.775
and gives you no hint why.

00:06:03.775 --> 00:06:06.514
Catch only the error you expect.

00:06:07.301 --> 00:06:11.049
Before we finish, a first look at lists and tuples.

00:06:11.049 --> 00:06:14.061
They store several values under one name.

00:06:14.411 --> 00:06:19.239
A list uses square brackets, and a tuple uses round brackets.

00:06:19.239 --> 00:06:23.751
You get one element out by its index, in square brackets,

00:06:23.751 --> 00:06:25.571
and indexes start at 0.

00:06:25.921 --> 00:06:32.801
So in the list [1, 2, 3], index 0 holds 1, and index 2 holds 3.

00:06:33.151 --> 00:06:37.377
The difference is that you can change a list, but not a tuple.

00:06:37.377 --> 00:06:40.240
Setting element 0 of the list to 10 works.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.511
Trying the same on the tuple raises a TypeError.

00:06:43.861 --> 00:06:45.421
A list is mutable.

00:06:45.421 --> 00:06:47.241
A tuple is immutable.

00:06:47.241 --> 00:06:51.141
Lists get a whole lecture of their own later.

00:06:51.935 --> 00:06:53.374
Let's put it together.

00:06:53.374 --> 00:06:58.804
Write a program that asks for a month and a day, and prints the date one day later.

00:06:58.804 --> 00:07:00.375
We'll ignore leap years.

00:07:00.725 --> 00:07:03.545
The number of days in each month never changes,

00:07:03.545 --> 00:07:05.045
so we keep it in a tuple.

00:07:05.395 --> 00:07:08.568
January is month 1, but index 0.

00:07:08.568 --> 00:07:14.915
So the length of the month is days in month, at index month - 1.

00:07:15.265 --> 00:07:21.040
If the day is before the end of the month, the answer is the next day of the same month.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.665
Otherwise, it's day 1 of the next month.

00:07:24.015 --> 00:07:26.532
The user types 2, then 28.

00:07:26.532 --> 00:07:31.278
February has 28 days, and 28 is not less than 28.

00:07:31.278 --> 00:07:36.895
So the else block runs, and the answer is 3, 1: March 1st.

00:07:37.245 --> 00:07:39.278
And December the 31st?

00:07:39.278 --> 00:07:41.865
12 mod 12 is 0, plus 1 is 1.

00:07:41.865 --> 00:07:47.965
So the month wraps around to January, and the program prints 1, 1.

00:07:48.735 --> 00:07:49.975
Let's sum up.

00:07:50.325 --> 00:07:52.991
Not, and, or combine conditions.

00:07:52.991 --> 00:07:55.825
Not goes first, then and, then or.

00:07:55.825 --> 00:08:00.325
And the last two stop as soon as they know the answer.

00:08:00.675 --> 00:08:03.748
Put a risky line in a short try block,

00:08:03.748 --> 00:08:08.115
and name the error you expect, like except ValueError.

00:08:08.465 --> 00:08:13.727
Lists and tuples hold several values, indexed from 0.

00:08:13.727 --> 00:08:15.514
A list can change.

00:08:15.514 --> 00:08:16.905
A tuple can't.

00:08:17.255 --> 00:08:18.846
Here's one to try.

00:08:18.846 --> 00:08:22.826
A year is a leap year if it's divisible by 4,

00:08:22.826 --> 00:08:26.628
except that years divisible by 100 are not,

00:08:26.628 --> 00:08:30.077
except that years divisible by 400 are.

00:08:30.077 --> 00:08:36.975
Write one Boolean expression, with no if, that is True exactly for leap years.

00:08:37.525 --> 00:08:41.811
Check it on 2024, 2000, 1900, and 2023.

00:08:41.811 --> 00:08:48.845
In part 3, we'll make programs repeat, with while and for loops.

